  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Employers and clients pay for fit between skill and need. They judge you by evidence, so your answers should lead to a trial and a sample.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is writing honest answers and comparing them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and self-questions before choosing a career: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools suggest careers from interests and summarise roles. Treat the output as a list to test, not an answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Self-knowledge, real trials and decisions you own hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI to generate options; later, test them with people and small projects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general self-questions before choosing a career path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Career test</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Quick list</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Limited depth</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Talking to a counsellor</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Personal view</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Quality varies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Shadowing</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Real view</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Hard to arrange</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn self-questions before choosing a career cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Self-questions before choosing a career is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free worksheets and checklists.</li>
    <li>Free YouTube day-in-the-life videos.</li>
    <li>Free public job notices.</li>
    <li>A teacher or senior who knows you.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tonight, answer the ten questions in writing and score two options.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">Ten questions in five groups</h2>

  <p>
    Answer each in one or two lines. Short answers are easier to compare later.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Group</th>
          <th>Questions</th>
          <th>Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Interest</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions">What do you do without being asked? What problems annoy you enough to fix?</td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Energy lasts longer than duty</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Strength</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions">What do others ask you for help with? What tasks feel easy to you?</td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Strength shortens learning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Values</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions">What do you want more of: security, freedom, impact or growth?</td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Values decide long-term fit</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Resources</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions">How much time, money and support can you count on?</td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">The plan must be affordable</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Group"><strong>Market</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions">Who pays for this work and what do they ask for in notices?</td>
          <td data-label="Why it matters">Demand shapes options</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="score">Turn answers into a simple comparison</h2>

  <p>
    Score each option from one to five on each group, then look at the lowest score, not only the total.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Option</th>
          <th>Interest</th>
          <th>Strength</th>
          <th>Values</th>
          <th>Resources</th>
          <th>Market</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Option"><strong>Option A</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Interest">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Strength">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Values">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Resources">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Market">Write 1 to 5</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Option"><strong>Option B</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Interest">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Strength">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Values">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Resources">Write 1 to 5</td>
          <td data-label="Market">Write 1 to 5</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A very low score in resources or market means you need a bridge plan, not a different dream.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money">Money: course fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree or course fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 8 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 80,000 for the main course, and a fifth is Rs 1.6 lakh as a serious caution. The rest can fund a laptop, practice time and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test the field with free courses and one finished project before paying for a long programme. Do not borrow heavily for a course whose graduates cannot show placement in a specific role.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this career</h2>

  <p>
    Test the choice with <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>: Biology, Context, Market and Survival.
  </p>
  <div class="protocol">
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Biology: what do you want your days to feel like?</div>
        <p>Write a description of a good ordinary day.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Context: time, money and home support</div>
        <p>Count time, money and support at home.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Market: who hires?</div>
        <p>Read ten notices for each option you are weighing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Survival: what survives AI?</div>
        <p>Lists of options are cheap. Tested choices hold value.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gate3">The 3 Gates: proof before commitment</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The 3 Gates</strong> check that your effort turns into work other people rely on.
  </p>
  <div class="gates-block">
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of reflection</span>
        <p>Keep your worksheet.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of trial</span>
        <p>Share what you tried.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of market</span>
        <p>List notice requirements.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Expensive mistakes</h2>

  <div class="mistake-list">
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Answering in your head only</div>
        <p>Write it down.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Using only marks or peers</div>
        <p>Add interest and values.</p>
      </div>
